          "create_time": 1792615144.63,
          "update_time": 1792615158.047,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "**We now have a much more explicit way to analyze a hypothetical multiple zero. The estimate that would rule it out remains unproved.**\n\nThe progress has three main parts:\n\n- **The local geometry is quantitatively controlled.** Assuming an exact double zero, we can construct a surrounding spacetime window, count its two roots, and bound its boundaries—even as the internal gap closes. This works separately at every finite height, with explicit dependence on the local derivatives. It removes a previously unpaid prerequisite without assuming simplicity.\n\n- **The source estimates preserve more of the relevant cancellation.** We derived how the discriminant—the quantity measuring squared root separation—responds to a source perturbation, with a rigorous nonlinear error bound. Using additional structure of the actual theta tail gives a strictly narrower signed interval than the matched pointwise envelope. **We have not shown that this narrower interval excludes zero.**\n\n- **The higher-multiplicity problem is better understood.** We derived an evolution law retaining the entire root cluster and its exterior contribution. Exact examples show why the two-root cap-sign argument cannot simply be reused: a quartic birth can have charge six despite equal positive cap signs. There is also a useful cubic result: a proper three-root cluster has at most one real collision time.\n\nThe main outstanding obstacles are:\n\n1. **A genuinely separating theta estimate.** We need a positive signed margin from the unchanged complete source, or an independently proved event location strictly before time zero. Good boundary bounds and small errors alone still permit a collision. This is the central mathematical obstacle.\n\n2. **Enough strength at every height.** The local windows may shrink with height; that is acceptable. What remains missing is a signed estimate that stays effective throughout those shrinking regimes. Another successful low-height calculation would not establish this.\n\n3. **Transfer to every multiplicity.** The many-root equations are available, but a successful double-zero estimate must still be extended to whole higher-order clusters. Treating their outgoing roots as isolated pairs would omit essential interactions.\n\nThe most useful next milestone is therefore a **strict, fully paid source margin near a potentially degenerating pair**, obtained without assuming the separation being proved. The current results have same-model internal review and exact algebra checks; independent specialist validation remains outstanding.\n\nThe detailed status is in the [progress register](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-3/13_contradiction_analysis/04_boundary_winding/threshold_routes/12_relative_chern_hopf_charges/research-progress/02_ENDPOINT_CHARGE_EXCLUSION_PROGRESS_20261021.md)."
            ]
          },
          "status": "finished_successfully",
          "end_turn": true,
